, which lost 29.8 points and left Black ahead by 32.1.", "moments": [{"turn": 14, "text": "G8 extended White's wall along the seventh and eighth rows, but it lost 13.6 points and moved Black's lead from +0.1 to +13.8. The engine preferred F6, in the middle, over reaching out to the side."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Black's F4 lost 15.9 points and turned a +11.9 lead into -4.1, putting White ahead for a moment. It was the high point of a messy exchange around G3, G4 and F3 in which both sides kept swinging the score."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "White's F2 looked like another quiet move in the bottom-right fight, yet it lost 20.4 points and sent Black's lead from +2.5 to +22.9. The game had been nearly even, so the fight along the bottom edge was effectively the whole game."}, {"turn": 51, "text": "The 20k gave it straight back: H1 lost 20.3 points, and the lead dropped from +22.9 to +2.5. The engine wanted H2, the point White then took on the next turn."}, {"turn": 54, "text": "J2 was the decisive blunder, losing 29.8 points and lifting Black's lead from +2.3 to +32.1. With Black stones on G1 and H1 and White's H2 stone in the corner, the engine's choice was F1. After that Black only had to finish the game."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran was helper code: game-044-inspection.js parsed each returned board and computed groups, liberties and empty regions, and logged them. game-044-verification.js replayed only the actual recorded moves to check boards and captures, and the remaining calls read or wrote notes, SGF and log files. Nothing looped over candidate moves, ranked them or played out continuations, so every move choice was made outside the code.", "stop": false}}